package jan-klod:interfaces@0.3.0;

/// One-way observation channel — an extension reports something notable and the
/// host decides what to do with it (log it today; metrics or a subscriber later).
///
/// **Publish only, deliberately.** This interface used to declare
/// `subscribe`/`next-event`/`unsubscribe` as well, backed by host stubs that
/// returned a handle and then `none` forever. Nothing published, nothing
/// subscribed, and a guest that started polling would have waited on an empty
/// queue for the life of the process with no error to explain it. The delivery
/// half comes back when something delivers: a bus with no deliverer is not a bus,
/// it is a promise.
///
/// `publish` is kept because it works — the host records it — and because a
/// gateway noticing its upstream has died wants somewhere to say so that is not a
/// log level.
interface host-event {
    /// Report an event. `topic` is dot-separated (e.g. `mcp.server.down`);
    /// `payload` is a JSON-encoded value.
    ///
    /// Fire-and-forget: there is no delivery guarantee and no reader to observe.
    /// An extension must not depend on anyone acting on this.
    publish: func(topic: string, payload: string);
}
